 in a much better position to  judge whether he should be in Moscow  today than than I am but the fact is  that the international community has  been outraged by the behavior of Russia  and the Syrian government regime over  the last five years and thank goodness  the appalling error of judgment that was  made by President Obama in saying that  there were red lines which could not be  crossed and then doing nothing when  those  lines were cross which sent a signal to  Assad and to Russia that they could do  what they want thank goodness the  President Trump you know I wouldn't have  voted for him had I been an American at  the last election but thank goodness he  has reasserted the importance of  international humanitarian law he said  that he will not stand for war crimes  being committed and has acted in the way  that he did so you're praising Donald  Trump for that decisive airstrike on the  other hand we have reports out today  that Boris Johnson is going to be trying  to get a strongly-worded a hard-hitting  statement about Russia from the g7 so  while America is backing military action  we're trying to get a strongly worded  letter I mean is that enough well  America is the one country that could  take the action which was taken I think  that President Trump was decisive I  think he was proportionate and I think  he was absolutely right to do what he  did and all of us actually not just the  poor people in the cities in Syria but  all of us around the world can sleep  more safely as a result of international  humanitarian law being reasserted and  the world's main super pass standing up  for that which they weren't before  should the UK be prepared then to back  the US and literally well the UK should  be prepared as in an alliance with our  allies with America hopefully with the  UN as well though that is extremely  difficult because of the veto structure  that exists there hopefully the  international community will now say  that we simply cannot stand by and allow  international humanitarian law to be  broken but let's be clear what is  required in Syria today is a ceasefire  unfettered access for humanitarian  agencies a ceasefire that we were told  would be brokered and secured by Russia  Iran and Turkey and that's what we now  need to see and the critical thing is to  hold the Russians to their commitment on  the ceasefire that they will police it  and deliver it in every way they  possibly can and not as I say be  passively acquiescent in regime attacks  on innocent civilians the difficulty is  though isn't it that in the Syrian  conflict that doesn't really seem to be  a good side to back you have Assad on  the one hand who by all accounts is  carrying out these devastating chemical  attacks on his own citizens  there on the other hand you have own  opposition that is heavily in  treated by Islamic state if you attack a  sad are you just helping Islamic state  what we need is this ceasefire so that  negotiations can take place those  negotiations need to be from the bottom  up you're quite right that there are  fifty or so different parties in this  conflict in one way or another and it's  not for us to decide what a peaceful  outcome should be but what we can say is  that we will not stand by and allow  innocent civilians and children to be  murdered in the way they have been in  Aleppo and in many other places in Syria  as well in this conflict that's gone on  for many years thousands of people died  children we've seen chemical attacks and  we've seen the appalling of events in  some of these cities how much  responsibility does the UK and UK  politicians bear for suntanning buying  for not backing for example in 2013  military action well in my view that was  a terrible error of judgment by the  House of Commons and I think you know  svet hindsight is a great thing but  those of us who were passionately in  favor of Britain standing up then  against these abuses of international  humanitarian law I I think I think were  right we weren't able to carry the day  we weren't able to persuade our  colleagues of the importance of that and  I think when we look back we will say  that the international community has  been supine in the face of the most  extraordinary barbarity that's taken  place in Syria and done nothing about it  but again I think a lot of the blame for  that  was that an American president said that  he would take action if red lines were  crossed chemical weapons were there news  for the first time in a in the sort of  state way since the 1920s and he did  nothing about it at the same time though  taking action now when the picture is  more complex and perhaps it was in 2013  that risks confrontation with Russia  doesn't it I mean things could get very  very messy indeed well I think this is a  proportionate reaction to what happened  and I think it sends a very clear  message to the Kremlin that they cannot  break international humanitarian law and  that's extremely important it's not a  declaration of war against Russia were  standing up for international  humanitarian principles okay Andrew  Mitchell thank you very much for your  thoughts today
 

